  nonce: 0a9c  # 0a9c
  message: "GATE RE-READ AGAINST A SMALLER NUMBER, and it moves further out of range.\n\nThis item is deferred behind a \"tens of minutes on a monorepo\" threshold. The\nmeasurement was 8.2 minutes; after the WI-balaf fix a cold full analysis of this\nmonorepo is 220.0s = 3.7 minutes. Per-file analyzer memoization is aimed at the pass\nblock, which is now 94.1s of that. So the gate is not merely unmet — the thing this\nitem would optimize shrank while the threshold stayed put.\n\nStaying deferred. WI-rukaz (a Java repo of ~14.6k files running >60min) remains the\ncandidate that would ungate it, and it should be measured with the phase-split\ninstrument rather than by subtraction, since a repo dominated by a tree-sitter\nlanguage exercises a different locate path (tree-sitter descends natively; it was the\nhand-written python walk that went quadratic)."  # 0a9c
- op: discuss  # e8b4
  at: '2026-08-17T18:31:44Z'  # e8b4
  by: agent  # e8b4
  actor: jgstern_agent  # e8b4
  clock: 4  # e8b4
  nonce: e8b4  # e8b4
  message: "CEILING MEASURED (2026-08-17), and it CONFIRMS the premise rather than refuting\nit — I expected fixed per-language costs to undercut this item and they do not.\n\nscripts/measure-analyzer-scaling.py calls run_all_analyzers directly (skipping\nthe ~93s of linkers + graph work that this question is not about), sweeping\nmax_files to vary per-file work while holding the language mix fixed.\n\n    max_files=1   analyzer phase wall  2.6s   (252 files still analyzed)\n    uncapped      analyzer phase wall 65.7s   (1385 files)\n    marginal python cost                56.3 ms/file (63.1s / 1120 files)\n\nFIXED COST IS <= 2.6s OF 65.7s, UNDER 4% — and even that 2.6s includes real\nper-file work for 251 non-python files, so the true grammar-load / registry /\ndispatch floor is lower still. The analyzer phase is ~96% per-file work.\n\nCONSEQUENCE FOR THIS ITEM. A per-file cache can win back essentially the whole\nphase: 65.7s -> ~3s when nothing changed. Against the current 160.9s cold\n`--minimal` analysis, that lands the run near 97s, a 1.7x cut. Real, but NOT the\nstep-change to the 4.5s warm number — because the remaining ~93s (linkers ~29s,\ngraph post-processing ~43.5s, ~20.3s still unattributed) is whole-graph work\nthat per-file memoization cannot touch. Anyone hoping this item makes the local\ncycle feel instant should read that sentence twice.\n\nTWO MEASUREMENT CAVEATS, both load-bearing:\n - The linear fit over the sweep returns a NEGATIVE intercept (-12.3s), i.e. the\n   model is wrong, because max_files does not bind uniformly (WI-fomid: bash,\n   markdown, json, toml and ~17 others ignore it, putting a floor of 252 files\n   under every point). The <=2.6s bound survives because it is a MEASURED\n   configuration, not an extrapolation. Do not quote the fit.\n - Per-pass duration_ms cannot be used to price this work at all (WI-nuvam): it\n   is per-pass WALL under a thread pool, so it inflates with contention. Same 43\n   bash files billed 2.3s or 49.9s depending only on what python was doing\n   alongside. Price with the phase-split instrument, never with card sums."  # e8b4
